    "items": [
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-finite-mode-occupation-basis",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Finite fermion-mode occupation basis",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\mathcal K$ be a finite-dimensional complex Hilbert space with a finite ordered orthonormal basis $(e_j)_{j\\in J}$, and use the antisymmetric Fock construction and second-variable-linear convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Assume its fermionic operators satisfy the CAR of [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For $S=\\{j_1<\\cdots<j_k\\}\\subseteq J$, set\n$$\\xi_S=c^\\dagger(e_{j_1})\\cdots c^\\dagger(e_{j_k})\\Omega,\\qquad \\xi_\\varnothing=\\Omega.$$\nThen $(\\xi_S)_{S\\subseteq J}$ is an orthonormal basis of $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$. For each $j\\in J$, the bounded number operator $N_j=c^\\dagger(e_j)c(e_j)$ is an orthogonal projection and\n$$N_j\\xi_S=\\mathbf1_{j\\in S}\\xi_S.$$\nConsequently, for any partition $J=\\bigsqcup_{x\\in V}J_x$ into finite site-mode sets, $n_x=\\sum_{j\\in J_x}N_j$ is bounded self-adjoint and acts on $\\xi_S$ by the occupation count $N_x(S)=|S\\cap J_x|\\in\\{0,\\ldots,|J_x|\\}$.\n\n",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979), §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696",
              "url": "https://chimera.roma1.infn.it/ENZO/FC/ESTRATTI/DUALITY/kogut1979.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Patrick Emonts and Erez Zohar, Gauss Law, Minimal Coupling and Fermionic PEPS for Lattice Gauge Theories, SciPost Physics Lecture Notes 12 (2020), §§2.1–2.4",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1807.01294"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": null,
        "reference": "Local A-page prerequisite: prove the finite-mode occupation ONB and site-number spectrum used by the exact Gauss multipliers.",
        "design_state": "Added after dependency audit; complete local conditional proof from the current Fock and CAR interfaces.",
        "inventory_expansion_required": true
      },
      {
        "id": "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Exact compact U(1) rotor plus finite CAR data",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q5; reserved proposed inventory, exact conditional statement authored in items/def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data.md",
        "design_state": "Current batch-37 QFT-MF01 Fock/CAR interfaces inspected and matched; model remains conditional on draft suppliers; finite occupation prerequisite supplied locally.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "lem-qft-finite-mode-occupation-basis",
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
          "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-qft-finite-mode-occupation-basis": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Gamma=(V,E,s,t,\\mathcal P)$ be a finite oriented graph with nonempty finite vertex set $V$, nonempty finite link set $E$, endpoint maps $s,t:E\\to V$, and a finite list $\\mathcal P$ of oriented simple cycles. Parallel links and self-loops are allowed. An oriented simple cycle $p\\in\\mathcal P$ is a finite closed path using each link at most once; write $\\sigma_{p,e}\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$ for its signed use of link $e$, so its signed incidence at every vertex is zero. Set $\\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$ with normalized measure $d\\theta/(2\\pi)$ and use product measure on $\\mathbb T^E$.\n\nFix an integer $s\\ge1$ and let $\\mathcal K=\\bigoplus_{x\\in V}\\mathbb C^s$ with orthonormal modes $e_{x\\alpha}$, $1\\le\\alpha\\le s$. This is finite-dimensional and hence separable. Use the antisymmetric Fock space and second-variable-linear inner product convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The current draft supplier [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]] states that, assuming Countable Choice, $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$ is a Hilbert space and its creation and annihilation operators are bounded and satisfy the CAR; that supplier is still a draft carrier, not an accepted result in this run. The finite-mode basis result [[lem-qft-finite-mode-occupation-basis]] constructs the joint orthonormal occupation basis and proves its number-operator eigenvalues from that exact CAR interface. Write $c_{x\\alpha}=c(e_{x\\alpha})$, $c^\\dagger_{x\\alpha}=c^\\dagger(e_{x\\alpha})$, and $n_x=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s c^\\dagger_{x\\alpha}c_{x\\alpha}$. On an occupation label $\\mathbf m=(m_{x\\alpha})$, each $m_{x\\alpha}\\in\\{0,1\\}$ and $n_x$ acts by $N_x(\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}$; it is bounded self-adjoint with spectrum contained in $\\{0,\\ldots,s\\}$.\n\nThe kinematical Hilbert space is\n$$\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}=L^2(\\mathbb T^E)\\widehat\\otimes\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K).$$\nThe coordinate Fourier characters $e_{\\mathbf n}(\\theta)=\\exp(i\\sum_{e\\in E}n_e\\theta_e)$, $\\mathbf n\\in\\mathbb Z^E$, form an orthonormal basis of the first factor by [[thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus]]. On this basis define the electric operator $E_e e_{\\mathbf n}=n_e e_{\\mathbf n}$, with its maximal diagonal domain; hence $E_e$ is self-adjoint with integer spectrum. Define the bounded unitary multiplication operator $U_e f(\\theta)=e^{i\\theta_e}f(\\theta)$, equivalently $U_e e_{\\mathbf n}=e_{\\mathbf n+\\delta_e}$, so $[E_e,U_f]=\\delta_{ef}U_f$ on $D(E_e)$.\n\nFix background integers $b_x\\in\\{0,\\ldots,s\\}$. In the joint basis define the real integer multiplier\n$$\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{e:s(e)=x}n_e-\\sum_{e:t(e)=x}n_e-\\left(\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}-b_x\\right),$$\nand let $G_x$ be its maximal diagonal operator, with domain $D(G_x)=\\{\\psi:\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)|^2|\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty\\}$. On the common link-electric domain this is the finite sum $\\sum_{s(e)=x}E_e-\\sum_{t(e)=x}E_e-(n_x-b_xI)$. These are the Gauss operators; the operator constraint defines $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}:=\\bigcap_{x\\in V}\\ker G_x$. For $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb T^V$ define the diagonal multiplier $U(\\alpha)=\\exp(i\\sum_x\\alpha_xG_x)$; the next lemma proves that these operators form a strongly continuous representation and that their normalized Haar average is the orthogonal projection onto $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$.\n\nFor each $x$, choose a Hermitian matrix $M_x\\in M_s(\\mathbb C)$, and for each link $e$ choose a matrix $T_e\\in M_s(\\mathbb C)$. Choose real coefficients $\\lambda_E>0$ and $\\lambda_p\\in\\mathbb R$ for $p\\in\\mathcal P$. Write\n$$W_p=\\prod_{e\\in E}U_e^{\\sigma_{p,e}},\\qquad V_B=-\\frac12\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\lambda_p(W_p+W_p^\\dagger),$$\nand\n$$V_F=\\sum_{x\\in V}\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta=1}^s c^\\dagger_{x\\alpha}(M_x)_{\\alpha\\beta}c_{x\\beta}+\\sum_{e\\in E}\\left(A_e+A_e^\\dagger\\right),\\qquad A_e=\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta=1}^s c^\\dagger_{s(e),\\alpha}(T_e)_{\\alpha\\beta}U_e c_{t(e),\\beta}.$$\nThe operator $H_0=\\lambda_E\\sum_{e\\in E}E_e^2$ is the diagonal multiplier with domain\n$$D(H_0)=\\left\\{\\psi:\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}\\left(\\lambda_E\\sum_{e\\in E}n_e^2\\right)^2|\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty\\right\\},$$\nwhere $\\mathbf m=(m_{x\\alpha})$ ranges over occupation configurations with each $m_{x\\alpha}\\in\\{0,1\\}$, and $N_x(\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}$ is the eigenvalue of $n_x$ on that basis vector. The coefficients $\\lambda_E,\\lambda_p$ and entries of $M_x,T_e$ are ordinary real or complex scalars here; no physical units are assigned. Set $H=H_0+V_B+V_F$ on $D(H_0)$. This is a mathematical finite-graph regulator definition. It asserts no physical adoption, continuum limit, Lorentz invariance, or identification with a particular continuum QED model.\n\nFor sign comparison only, if a smooth path $\\gamma_e:[0,1]\\to X$ in a smooth manifold $X$, a real $C^1$ connection one-form $a$, and a real $C^1$ function $\\lambda$ are supplied, take $U_e^{\\rm cont}=\\exp(-ig\\int_0^1a_{\\gamma_e(r)}(\\dot\\gamma_e(r))\\,dr)$ for real $g$. Under $a\\mapsto a+d\\lambda$, the endpoint factor is $e^{ig(\\lambda(s(e))-\\lambda(t(e)))}$, matching the finite vertex-phase convention when $\\alpha_x=g\\lambda(x)$. This comparison supplies no embedding of the finite graph into $X$ and asserts no continuum limit.\n\n",
        "strategy": "Define the finite graph, link torus, CAR Fock factor, maximal diagonal Gauss operators, bounded plaquette and hopping terms, and exact electric Hamiltonian domain. Preserve the conditional QFT-MF01 CAR supplier and the fixed-regulator scope.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
