      {
        "id": "lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Gauss operators, gauge group and nonempty physical projection",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q5; reserved proposed inventory, exact conditional statement authored in items/lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection.md",
        "design_state": "reserved claim; complete conditional argument authored; Step-1 readiness escalated pending batch-37 carrier",
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the conditional compact-rotor/CAR data of [[def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data]]. In the joint Fourier/occupation basis write\n$$\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{e:s(e)=x}n_e-\\sum_{e:t(e)=x}n_e-\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}+b_x\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\nFor every vertex $x$, let $G_x$ be multiplication by $\\gamma_x$ on its maximal diagonal domain\n$$D(G_x)=\\left\\{\\psi:\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)|^2|\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThen each $G_x$ is self-adjoint, the $G_x$ strongly commute, and $U(\\alpha)=\\exp(i\\sum_x\\alpha_xG_x)$ is a strongly continuous unitary representation of $\\mathbb T^V$. Its action obeys\n$$U(\\alpha)c_{x\\beta}U(\\alpha)^\\dagger=e^{i\\alpha_x}c_{x\\beta},\\qquad U(\\alpha)U_eU(\\alpha)^\\dagger=e^{i(\\alpha_{s(e)}-\\alpha_{t(e)})}U_e.$$\nThe normalized Haar average\n$$P=\\frac1{(2\\pi)^{|V|}}\\int_{[0,2\\pi]^{|V|}}U(\\alpha)\\,d\\alpha$$\nis the orthogonal projection onto $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}=\\bigcap_x\\ker G_x$. This subspace is nonzero for every admissible fixed background $b$: it contains the normalized state with link Fourier labels $n_e=0$ and site occupations $m_{x\\alpha}$ chosen so $\\sum_\\alpha m_{x\\alpha}=b_x$.\n\n",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
