              "url": "https://chimera.roma1.infn.it/ENZO/FC/ESTRATTI/DUALITY/kogut1979.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Patrick Emonts and Erez Zohar, Gauss Law, Minimal Coupling and Fermionic PEPS for Lattice Gauge Theories, SciPost Physics Lecture Notes 12 (2020), §§2.1–2.4",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1807.01294"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-lattice-hamiltonian-selfadjoint",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Rotor interacting Hamiltonian with exact domain",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q5; reserved proposed inventory, exact conditional statement authored in items/lem-qft-lattice-hamiltonian-selfadjoint.md",
        "design_state": "reserved claim; complete conditional argument authored; Step-1 readiness escalated pending batch-37 carrier",
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the model data of [[def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data]]. The nonnegative electric operator $H_0=\\lambda_E\\sum_e E_e^2$ is self-adjoint on the stated maximal diagonal domain and finite Fourier/occupation sums are a graph core. The finite plaquette and fermion interaction $V=V_B+V_F$ is bounded and self-adjoint. Therefore $H=H_0+V$ is self-adjoint on exactly $D(H_0)$. Moreover $H$ commutes with every gauge transformation $U(\\alpha)$ on this domain. No continuum-limit assertion follows.\n\n",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979), §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696",
