      {
        "id": "lem-qft-qed-gauss-localization",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Gauss law and charged localization obstruction",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q6; reserved proposed inventory, exact conditional statement authored in items/lem-qft-qed-gauss-localization.md",
        "design_state": "reserved claim; complete conditional argument authored; Step-1 readiness escalated pending batch-37 carrier",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "lem-qft-lattice-local-gauss-flux"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-qft-lattice-local-gauss-flux": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the exact finite-graph model and let $R\\subseteq V$. Set $Q_R=\\sum_{x\\in R}(n_x-b_xI)$ and let $\\partial R$ and its orientation signs be as in [[lem-qft-lattice-local-gauss-flux]]. On $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}\\cap\\bigcap_{e\\in\\partial R}D(E_e)$, suppose a bounded operator $O$ preserves $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$ and, for every boundary link $e$, maps $D(E_e)$ into itself and satisfies $E_eO\\phi=OE_e\\phi$ for every $\\phi\\in D(E_e)$. Then $[Q_R,O]\\psi=0$ for every vector in that physical domain. Consequently, a charge-changing operator with $[Q_R,O]\\psi=qO\\psi$ for a fixed $q\\ne0$ cannot both preserve the physical sector and commute with the boundary electric operators while acting nontrivially on any such physical vector.\n\nThere is also the following conditional continuum statement; it does not assert that a continuum representation exists. Let $D$ be a common invariant dense domain in a Hilbert space, and let $\\rho(f)$ and $E_k(g_k)$ be linear assignments from real-valued test functions $f,g_k\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ to operators $D\\to D$. For a real vector test function $\\mathbf g=(g_1,g_2,g_3)\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ set $E(\\mathbf g)=\\sum_{k=1}^3E_k(g_k)$. Assume the assignments satisfy on $D$\n$$\\rho(f)=-\\varepsilon_0\\sum_{k=1}^3E_k(\\partial_k f)\\quad(f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)),$$\nfor a fixed $\\varepsilon_0>0$, and let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be compact. Suppose $O$ is bounded, maps $D$ into itself, and satisfies $E(\\mathbf g)O\\psi=OE(\\mathbf g)\\psi$ for every $\\psi\\in D$ whenever $\\operatorname{supp}\\mathbf g$ is disjoint from $K$. For each integer $R\\ge R_0$, choose a real-valued $\\chi_R\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ equal to $1$ on a neighborhood of $K$ and with $\\operatorname{supp}\\nabla\\chi_R$ disjoint from $K$, and put $Q_R=\\rho(\\chi_R)=-\\varepsilon_0 E(\\nabla\\chi_R)$. Assume there is an operator $Q:D\\to D$ such that, for every $\\psi\\in D$, both $Q_R\\psi\\to Q\\psi$ and $Q_RO\\psi\\to QO\\psi$ as integer $R\\to\\infty$. Then $[Q,O]\\psi=0$ on $D$. A bounded charge-changing operator satisfying $[Q,O]\\psi=qO\\psi$ for $q\\ne0$ on $D$ must therefore be the zero operator. These continuum conclusions are conditional on the displayed domain, locality, convergence and commutator-passage assumptions.\n\n",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979), §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696",
              "url": "https://chimera.roma1.infn.it/ENZO/FC/ESTRATTI/DUALITY/kogut1979.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Patrick Emonts and Erez Zohar, Gauss Law, Minimal Coupling and Fermionic PEPS for Lattice Gauge Theories, SciPost Physics Lecture Notes 12 (2020), §§2.1–2.4",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1807.01294"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-lattice-ward-contact",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Time-ordered Ward contact relation",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q6; reserved proposed inventory, exact conditional statement authored in items/lem-qft-lattice-ward-contact.md",
        "design_state": "reserved claim; complete conditional argument authored; Step-1 readiness escalated pending batch-37 carrier",
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data",
          "lem-qft-lattice-ward-identities",
          "lem-qft-lattice-physical-evolution",
          "def-axiom-of-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-lattice-ward-identities": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-lattice-physical-evolution": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the compact finite-graph model and the Axiom of Choice. Fix a vertex $x$, pairwise distinct real times $t_1,\\ldots,t_n$ with $n\\ge1$, bounded operators $O_j$, and a normalized physical vector $\\psi$. Assume each $O_j$ maps $D(G_x)$ into itself and has a bounded commutator with $G_x$. Let\n$$O_j(t_j)=\\mathcal U(t_j)^\\dagger O_j\\mathcal U(t_j),\\qquad G_x(t)=\\mathcal U(t)^\\dagger G_x\\mathcal U(t),$$\nwhere $\\mathcal U(t)=e^{-itH/\\hbar}$. Define ordinary time ordering by placing the factor with the larger time to the left; $G_x$ is bosonic, so moving it past an insertion introduces no grading sign. Then $G_x(t)=G_x$, and, as a distribution in $t$,\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\left\\langle\\psi,\\mathcal T\\!\\left[G_x(t)\\prod_{j=1}^nO_j(t_j)\\right]\\psi\\right\\rangle=\\sum_{j=1}^n\\delta(t-t_j)\\left\\langle\\psi,\\mathcal T\\!\\left([G_x,O_j(t_j)]\\prod_{i\\ne j}O_i(t_i)\\right)\\psi\\right\\rangle.$$\nThe products and commutators are evaluated on the common domains just specified. This is a finite-regulator contact identity, not a claim about continuum renormalized time-ordered products.\n\n",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979), §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696",
              "url": "https://chimera.roma1.infn.it/ENZO/FC/ESTRATTI/DUALITY/kogut1979.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Patrick Emonts and Erez Zohar, Gauss Law, Minimal Coupling and Fermionic PEPS for Lattice Gauge Theories, SciPost Physics Lecture Notes 12 (2020), §§2.1–2.4",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1807.01294"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      }
    ]
